// `stringify` and `parse` checked against EACH OTHER rather than against a
// golden line someone chose: build one value using all six `JsonValue`
// variants, render it, parse the rendering, render again, and compare the two
// texts. A one-sided bug -- an escape the encoder writes and the decoder
// misreads, or the reverse -- shows up here as an inequality even if both
// goldens elsewhere agree with the code that produced them.
//
// The value is deliberately awkward in every way the two directions can
// disagree:
//
//   * an array nested inside an object, and an object nested inside that array;
//   * an empty array and an empty string, both of which take a scanner's
//     early-exit path rather than its loop;
//   * a non-integral number (`2.5`) and one whose text needs the fraction
//     digits kept (`-0.001`);
//   * a string carrying a quote, a backslash, a line feed, a tab, and a control
//     character with no short escape -- `\u0001`, which is the only one that
//     goes through `\uXXXX` in both directions.
//
// EVERY object here has exactly one key, and that restriction protects THIS
// FIXTURE'S PRIMARY ASSERTION, not merely the determinism of its golden.
// `Map::keys()` returns table order, and re-inserting keys in emitted slot
// order can CHANGE that order: when two keys share a home slot, linear probing
// puts whichever arrives second in the forward slot, so emitting and reparsing
// swaps which one probes and the slot order flips. Measured over all 3782
// ordered pairs of single-character keys from `[a-zA-Z0-9]`: 56 of them give
// `first != second`, and all 56 are stable 2-cycles in which a third render
// equals the first. Inserting `"p"` then `"h"` is one -- it renders
// `{"h":1,"p":2}`, whose reparse renders `{"p":2,"h":1}`, whose reparse renders
// the first again. NO `grow` IS INVOLVED: two entries never approach a cap-8
// table's 3/4 threshold. So `first == second` would simply FAIL for 1.5% of
// two-key objects; a golden pinning a hash ordering is the weaker of the two
// reasons, not the whole one.
//
// THE FIGURES ABOVE WERE MEASURED UNDER THE UNSEEDED `str_hash`: the 56 of
// 3782, the 1.5% that follows from it, and `"p"` then `"h"` rendering
// `{"h":1,"p":2}`. `str_hash` is now seeded once per process, so which pairs
// flip is a function of the layout a run happens to build and none of them
// may be relied on run to run -- not that each must differ, since two seeds may
// agree on a layout. They are kept rather than deleted because what they
// establish survives: a two-key object's rendered order can flip across a
// render and a reparse with NO `grow` involved, so the one-key restriction is
// what makes `first == second` a property of the codec rather than of the run.
//
// The printed text was derived from `stringify`'s own
// rules and Rust's `f64` Display, not captured -- `0.0` renders `0`, `1.0`
// renders `1`, and `quote` escapes `"` `\` LF TAB as `\"` `\\` `\n` `\t` while
// leaving `q` alone and sending `0x01` to `\u0001`.
//
// The second comparison is idempotence: parsing and re-rendering the rendering
// must be a fixed point. A decoder that lost a character would fail the first
// comparison; one that ADDED a character on every pass would pass the first and
// fail this one.
fn main() {
    let mut deep: Map<String, JsonValue> = Map::new()
    deep.insert("d", Number(1.0))

    let none: [JsonValue] = []
    let inner: [JsonValue] = [Number(1.0), Array([Number(2.0)])]

    let items: [JsonValue] = [
        Null,
        Bool(true),
        Bool(false),
        Number(0.0),
        Number(2.5),
        Number(-0.001),
        String("q\"\\\n\t\u{0001}"),
        String(""),
        Array(none),
        Array(inner),
        Object(deep),
    ]

    let mut root: Map<String, JsonValue> = Map::new()
    root.insert("root", Array(items))

    let first = stringify(Object(root))
    match parse(first) {
        Ok(v) => {
            let second = stringify(v)
            println("first == second -> ${first == second}")
            match parse(second) {
                Ok(w) => println("second == third -> ${second == stringify(w)}")
                Err(e) => println("second == third -> error: ${e.msg} at ${e.at}")
            }
            println(second)
        }
        Err(e) => println("first == second -> error: ${e.msg} at ${e.at}")
    }
}
